First Author: Dávalos-Yerovi
Title: Factors Influencing the Attrition Rate of a 10-Week Multimodal Rehabilitation Program in Patients After Lung Transplant: A Neural Network Analysis
Source number: 11  ￼

Very short summary: This prospective observational study used an artificial neural network to identify baseline factors linked to attrition during a 10-week multimodal rehabilitation program after lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 41 first-time lung transplant recipients were included; (2) 17/41 patients (41.4%) had a high attrition rate; (3) only 23 baseline variables without missing data were entered into the ANN; (4) the strongest determinants of high attrition were low age-dependent BMI, lung-transplant indication, lower health-related quality of life, and lower handgrip strength; and (5) the cohort had a high prevalence of malnutrition (80.5%) and sarcopenia (56.1%), but these newer consensus-based syndromic definitions could not be fully tested in the ANN because of missing data constraints. Tables and figures used directly here include Table 1, Table 2, and Figures 2–5.

